 ## Task progress reporting
 
-A task/batch progress API — live counts, percentages, an event stream, or heartbeats — is not added. `map`/`runAll` execute synchronously on the calling thread and the public surface exposes no running handle, so the only observation point is scope exit, which already returns frozen terminal observations (`TaskExitObservation`, `TaskGroupResult`). A live progress stream would serve only a thread watching from outside the scope, the interaction shape structured concurrency removes by design, and it would become a third observability mechanism beside the existing reports (starvation observation, workload-fit screening, executor wait graph) with its own subscription, replay, backpressure, and observer-executor contract. Progress is also business semantics: only the task code knows what the total and each increment mean, so publishing counters from inside the task function to your own metrics backend is both the supported extension point and the more accurate model. A primary-source survey of progress mechanisms across twelve systems (2026-11-12) confirmed the contract surface this would require; the direction is closed by explicit user decision (2026-11-15) and recorded in `design/decision-log.md`.
+A task/batch progress API — live counts, percentages, an event stream, or heartbeats — is not added. `map`/`runAll` execute synchronously on the calling thread and the public surface exposes no running handle, so the only observation point is scope exit, which already returns frozen terminal observations (`TaskExitObservation`, `TaskGroupResult`). A live progress stream would serve only a thread watching from outside the scope, the interaction shape structured concurrency removes by design, and it would become a third observability mechanism beside the existing reports (starvation observation, executor wait graph) with its own subscription, replay, backpressure, and observer-executor contract. Progress is also business semantics: only the task code knows what the total and each increment mean, so publishing counters from inside the task function to your own metrics backend is both the supported extension point and the more accurate model. A primary-source survey of progress mechanisms across twelve systems (2026-11-12) confirmed the contract surface this would require; the direction is closed by explicit user decision (2026-11-15) and recorded in `design/decision-log.md`.
 
 ## Automatic cancelled-task purge
 
 The library once purged cancelled tasks from user executor queues automatically; the feature was removed in 0.3.0. A cancelled `Future` can stay referenced in a `ThreadPoolExecutor` queue until a worker consumes it, but making the library clean that up required a per-executor maintenance protocol on the latency-sensitive cancellation path — coalescing delays, admission thresholds, and ordering guarantees that cost more than the stale references they removed. Queue cleanup belongs to the executor's owner: call `ThreadPoolExecutor.purge()` at your own maintenance boundary. The event-coalesced purge design is preserved on the `dev/experimental` branch should automatic purge ever return.
 
+## Workload declarations and blocking detection (`TaskType`)
+
+Version 0.4 let tasks and entries declare a `TaskType` (`COMPUTE`, `BLOCKING`, `UNKNOWN`), logged a warning when the two declarations disagreed, and timed executions on `COMPUTE` entries with thread CPU time to flag blocking work. All of it was removed for 0.5.0. The comparison only caught a task whose author knew it blocked and still wired it to the wrong pool, while the common incident is blocking the author does not know about, which carries no declaration. The CPU-time screen was a heuristic that GC, safepoints, and CPU-quota throttling skew, with uncalibrated thresholds and a timing cost inside every screened execution. Enforcing the declaration instead would refuse honest declarations, since a compute task that waits for a nested batch is `BLOCKING`, and would turn a wrong label into a failed request. To keep a pool-purpose check, give each pool's `Par` its own application type so that wiring the wrong pool fails to compile; to find blocking calls on compute threads, run a blocking-call detector such as BlockHound in tests or staging. Reopen only with a blocking signal that is cheap enough to leave on and reliable without per-environment calibration (ADR 0016).
+
 ## What will not be added
 
 The project will stay focused on structured batch execution, cooperative cancellation, bounded scheduling, context propagation, and executor deadlock diagnostics. Features outside that boundary should be integrated explicitly rather than hidden in the core API.
